{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/467054-sooooo-how-do-you-do-it","id":467054,"title":"Sooooo... how do you do it?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Promethean: The Created"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-02T23:43:34-07:00","id":467054,"title":"Sooooo... how do you do it?","content":"I'm just getting back in to the nWoD, and I just realized something: I have no idea how to run a game of Promethean for more than one player. I can think up enough story and campaign ideas for a solo game, but for more than one of the Created? <br /> <br /> How do you do it? What kind of stories do you run?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/373-infernal-teddy","name":"Infernal Teddy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar373_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-03T04:50:26-07:00","id":467216,"title":"","content":"I'm not a huge scholar of Promethean, but given that two of the central themes are those of attaining humanity and alchemy, I feel like that could easily mesh with group play. Make Milestones that hinge on themes of man as a political animal being important to their progress towards humanity, working together and keeping companionship / relationships / friendships. Likewise, you could play up the alchemy side of things and emphasize the diverse elements (in this case the different PCs) that go into alchemical processes to work miracles... and that sometimes those reactions are volatile, but that the result is no less pure.<br /> <br /> Those are my thoughts, but I'd be interested to hear more from someone who has actual run a game of Promethean for a party.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3081-anachronistic-jam","name":"Anachronistic Jam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3081_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"211"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-03T07:45:59-07:00","id":467384,"title":"","content":"Play it as episodic. Focus on a different character in each game. Let them stumble upon good occasions. Create milestones in such a way that no matter what happens (or almost) then someone gets a Milestone. There's plenty of ways to do it, but 3 players is the best in my opinion <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Glorious Useless-Post Prana<br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Cost: 2</span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">m; </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Mins: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Linguistics 1, Essence 1 </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Type: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Simple</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Keywords: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Mute</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Duration: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Instant</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Prerequisite Charms: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Ten-Characters Methodology</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2589-magnum-opus","name":"Magnum Opus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2589_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"821"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-03T14:51:37-07:00","id":467890,"title":"","content":"I have run the same chronicle twice, for about 7-10 sessions each. I constructed the chronicle around an unseen Promethean's Pilgrimage, rather than that of any member of the party. This Promethean was old, probably 80 years of existence, and was something of a failure as he tended to focus on the metaphysics of the Pyros, rather than its purpose. Still, he managed to have a great deal of influence, and managed to gather a huge collection of Created in one space, for nearly a year, before the powers that be came crashing down on the camp. The prelude, aftermath, and diaspora of this event was the stage I had set for the game.<br /> <br /> What I found is that the Pilgrimage is a powerful core for a chronicle, but it has a clear end and beginning, and is subjective to each character. This can be constraining,since it is also a huge part of the player experience. I have a few suggestions with this in mind.<ul><li>You can do as I did, and make the key Pilgrimage an NPC Promethean's. The throng has their milestones, and the narrative core includes their journey, and some might even be Redeemed at some point, but the throng's <i>story</i> is bounded and structured by this third party's Redemption. This allows you to hit the thematic points of Promethean, and allow some characters the chance to complete their stories, but in a manner that spotlights each character equally. Characters who Redeem out can continue as human, or be replaced by new Prometheans, with the expectation that those characters character continue their journey after the end of the chronicle.</li> <li>You can make one player character's Pilgrimage the chronicle spine. This puts the Pilgrimage front and center, but has the problematic side effect of handing that player and character the keys to the chronicle. This is a spotlight issue too, because the chronicle revolves around the character, and other characters become secondary. Still, it has many of the previous suggestion's strengths and features.</li> <li>Magnum Opus (the commenter, not the supplement necessarily) has a good idea, to structure it episodically. This solves the spotlighting issue and keeps it on player characters. I suspect if there was a census of P:tC play, that would be the most common structure, as it's just good practice for group roleplaying to begin with. I'd also add that you might want to add flashbacks and epilogues. LOST and Orange Is The New Black are great examples of this sort of storytelling, and can help you convey the enormity of these interlinked Pilgrimages and how they play out over time. This approach sort of requires you to plan a lot of the chronicle out in advance, and have to be able to play well with continuity problems, but could be extremely rewarding: imagine a scene where a Redeemed player character encounters a throngmate some time later, his friend still Created and struggling.</li> <li>Tie the Pilgrimages together. If one Redeems, we all Redeem. There is some sort of mystical connection. I personally find the trope tired, that characters all start the game joined at the hip, but the structure is useful for the default chronicle, and I neglected to mention that I used a joint Generative Act to begin the chronicle(s) above. If they begin together, they may need to end together.</li> <li>There are more pressing concerns. The Pilgrimage is put on the back-burner, because an ominous threat must be dealt with first. This shortchanges the Promethean condition, but it does allow for freer play and story construction, and it can be combined with any of the above structures.</li> <li>The whole shebang. Every surviving character gets to the end of his Night. This is bulky and awkward, but I think it's an expectation players go in with if you don't talk about alternate structures, and it's probably the one worrying you right now. It's definitely the messiest to structure, and it can go off the rails if you don't have a way to keep the characters together and on the same path, but it could be the surest way to get the same players back for a second chronicle, if it floats your boat.</li> </ul>What's most important is that you talk to your players and find out what they want. Promethean puts a high importance on personal experience and desire, so catering to your players' playstyles and expressed expectations will keep them engaged, and part of that is tooling your concept for that audience.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>LFP: Foreign Bodies (Deviant: the Renegades)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/969-verge","name":"Verge","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T16:35:29-07:00","id":486829,"title":"","content":"How would you run a Promethean game for one player? Could some of your ideas apply to two characters? I wondered for a long time about why a throng might stay together. Overall, even with the threat of torment or multiple sources of disquiet, Prometheans are so rare that a throng has a powerful reason to stay with one another. The Created have no one else they can trust but each other. Three Prometheans can keep each other on the Pilgrimage, defend each other from Pandorans, and keep the mobs of humans away. If one of the throng suffers from torment, the others can mitigate the damage he would later regret.<br /> <br /> With multiple players, look at running the chronicle as a group taking long journey with one another, throwing different events at them and seeing the different reactions of each character as a result. It is kind of like Easy Rider through a mirror darkly, as the characters are on a pilgrimage of their own, and suffer persecution much like disquiet. Give players a destination in mind, and obstacles depending on how they get there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4725-murder_of_craws","name":"Murder_of_Craws","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4725_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"36"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-09T23:36:00-07:00","id":509649,"title":"","content":"I'm currently in the process of getting my first Promethean game in years together. Figure I'll share my setup:<br /> <br /> - I took a page from BlackHatMatt and created a questionnaire for my players to answer about their characters, which include everything from what they know to what they want to happen in game.<br /> - Once I have them fill out the questionnaire, we make the character mechanically.<br /> - Take a gander of all the mechanics and answers, and see what are the interesting bits. One character may have a &quot;Fresh Body.&quot; That implies something about the story. Another may have a &quot;famous face.&quot; Another &quot;Contacts.&quot; Someone may be Stannum, so you know they need a reason to gain hope. All of these let you flesh out a world and create a story.<br /> - I created an Excel sheet that has each character, their lineage and philosophy, then all their planned milestones based on the questionnaire and merits and such. I as a rule have three milestones that all prometheans have: Get an Athanor, Create a Promethean, and something to do with their Creator. Every Promethean's story is different, so that last one should always be tailored to the character.<br /> <br /> I find that the more prep work you do early, and the more involved you are with the character creation, helping them make exactly what they want, the better you can weave the story together.<br /> <br /> My two cents.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5985-jessel","name":"Jessel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5985_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}